course: arts
lesson: 31
title: Crafts: paper, clay, sewing, knitting, slime
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Crafts are art you make with your hands from real stuff: paper folded into cranes, clay squeezed into pots, thread sewn into buttons, yarn knitted into scarves, and glue turned into slime.
explain: Each material has its own rules: clay shrinks as it dries, flour paste goes mouldy, paper needs sharp creases, and fabric needs sharp scissors kept just for cloth.
explain: When a craft goes wrong, it is usually a rule of the material, not a lack of "craft hands": a clay handle that drops off was only pressed on, not joined properly.
explain: Some craft tools bite: needles prick, hot glue guns burn, candle wax for egg painting is hot and some slime mixes make skin sore, so those jobs are done with an adult close by.
explain: People say it their own way: "well mint" is northern English for really great, "sweet as" in Australia means fine, "wee" in Scotland means small, and "deadly" in Ireland means brilliant.
fact: Origami is the Japanese-rooted art of folding paper into shapes without cutting or gluing; sharp creases and corners lined up exactly make the model neat.
fact: Chinese paper cutting, called jianzhi, is a folk art listed by UNESCO; folding red paper before cutting makes the pattern repeat, and skilled cutters work by hand.
fact: Clay pieces join best when both surfaces are scratched, a little runny clay called slip is added, and they are pressed and smoothed together.
fact: Very thick lumps of clay can trap water or air that turns to steam in the kiln, a very hot oven, and crack the piece; potters keep walls even and not too thick.
fact: Some slime recipes use activators that can make skin red and sore; slime is made with an adult, never eaten, and washed off hands afterwards.
fact: Most needle pricks come from pushing the needle towards your own fingers; a thimble protects the pushing finger, and needles go back in a pincushion.
fact: People of all ages knit; the British Olympic diver Tom Daley was filmed knitting between events at the Tokyo Olympics in 2021.
fact: Papier-mâché paste made of flour and water is food, so it goes mouldy if left wet for days; mix only what you need.
fact: A hot glue gun melts glue sticks, and the tip and fresh glue are hot enough to burn, so children use it only with an adult.
fact: Ukrainian pysanky are made by drawing patterns in wax, dyeing the egg, and repeating from light to dark colours; the wax keeps the dye off.
fact: Fabric scissors are kept only for cloth, because cutting paper or card makes the blades blunt.
exercise: Mykola's crane looks like a squashed pigeon. What helps most? | Exact corners and sharp creases | Use thick card instead of thin paper so the crane stands up better | Fold much faster so the paper does not have time to slip or move | Glue it at the end
exercise: How are the two red paper fish cut by Li Na's grandma? | By hand, from folded paper | With a special printer that cuts out shapes from coloured paper | With a laser cutting machine in a paper factory in the city | By tearing the paper
exercise: What is jianzhi? | Chinese paper cutting | Japanese paper folding | Ukrainian egg painting | Scottish knitting
exercise: Ava's clay handle fell off. How should she join clay next time? | Scratch both sides, add slip, press and smooth | Just squish it on hard while it is wet | Use sticky tape to hold it | Let both pieces dry first, then push them together
exercise: Dmytro's mum warns his thick clay dragon might crack in the kiln. Why? | Trapped water becomes steam | Thick clay is far too heavy for the kiln shelf and will snap it | Dragon shapes always crack in kilns because of their long tails | Kilns are too cold
exercise: Sienna's hands go red and itchy from slime. What should she do? | Stop, wash and tell an adult | Keep squeezing the slime until her hands get used to it and stop hurting | Add more activator to the slime, because that usually fixes the itching | Lick her fingers
exercise: Callum stabs his thumb sewing a button. What helps? | Sew away from his fingers | Push the needle towards his thumb so he can feel where it goes | Use a much bigger and blunter needle that cannot go through cloth | Hold the needle in his mouth when resting so he does not lose it
exercise: Jack laughs that knitting is "for nans". Which is true? | People of all ages knit | Only grandmothers are able to learn knitting, as it takes years | Knitting was banned for boys in Britain until quite recently | It is only for girls
exercise: Hannah's flour paste is furry after three days. Why? | Flour paste is food, so it moulds | The newspaper strips slowly turned into fur as they dried out | Every kind of paste grows fur after just one hour in a bowl | Paste freezes overnight
exercise: Arjun wants to use the hot glue gun alone. What is right? | Only with an adult: it burns | It is only glue, so it is completely safe for anyone of any age | Touch the metal tip quickly first to check it has warmed up | Leave it plugged in on the carpet so it is ready next time
exercise: How is the pattern on Sofiia's pysanka made? | With wax that blocks dye, layer by layer | With a fine brush and paint | With stickers from a shop | With a printer that prints on eggs
exercise: Max cuts felt with his mum's fabric scissors. Why is she upset? | They are kept just for cloth | Felt is too strong for any pair of scissors and breaks them | All scissors are exactly the same, so she is just being fussy | She needs them for paper
exercise: What is a kiln? | A very hot oven for firing clay | A tool for pushing a needle | A Japanese paper fold | A runny clay glue
exercise: What is slip? | Runny clay used like glue | A fall on a wet floor | A kind of slime | A sewing stitch
exercise: Which dyes go first when making a pysanka? | Light colours first, then darker ones | Dark colours first, then lighter | Black first, then white | All colours at once
exercise: Where should pins and needles go after use? | In a pincushion | On the floor beside you | In your mouth to hold | In your pocket loose
exercise: What is origami? | Folding paper into shapes | Cutting red paper into repeating patterns for windows | Making pots and bowls out of soft clay | Painting eggs with wax and dye in many layers
test: Your mate goes, "Some people just have origami hands, I don't, so I quit." What is the best answer? | It is a skill you practise | He is right, neat folding is a gift that you are either born with or not | Paper is made to be almost impossible to fold neatly by hand | Only people who grew up in Japan are able to do origami well
test: What would happen if you left a bowl of flour paste out for a week? | It would go mouldy and smell | It would turn into stronger glue | It would turn into paper | It would stay fresh for months
test: A kid from Texas says, "Y'all, slime's just glue, it can't hurt nobody." What is true? | Some activators make skin sore | Slime is safe to eat in small bits because it is made of glue | Glue cannot hurt your skin at all, whatever it is mixed with | Shop slime is never glue
test: In a cartoon, a boy makes a solid clay brick dragon and it comes out of the kiln perfect. What would usually go wrong? | Thick clay can crack in the kiln | Nothing at all, because solid clay is always the strongest kind | The dragon would melt into a puddle of clay on the kiln shelf | It would turn to glass
test: Your little brother picks up a glue gun and says, "It's only glue, innit." What do you say? | It burns, get an adult | It is fine as long as he does it very quickly and carefully | Glue guns are toys made for kids, so he can use it by himself | He should touch the tip first to see if it is working properly
test: Your Irish friend calls Sofiia's egg "deadly" ("deadly" means brilliant). How did she avoid smudging? | Wax blocked the dye | She painted the lines with a tiny brush very fast before it dried | She bought it from a shop already decorated and just signed it | She used a computer printer and a sheet of shiny stickers
test: Callum says "that wee needle's out to get me" ("wee" means small). Which is true? | Pricks come from sewing towards your fingers | Needles move by themselves when you are not looking at them | Only clumsy people ever get pricked when they sew buttons | Thimbles cause pricks
test: Ava says her clay join was "sweet as" yesterday ("sweet as" means fine). Why did it fall off? | Drying clay shrank and pulled apart | Clay always falls apart in the end, whatever you do to join it | Someone in the cupboard pulled the handle off during the night | The kiln was too hot
test: Jack teases Rohan for knitting on the bus. What is the wise move for Rohan? | Keep knitting, let jokes slide | Stop knitting for ever so that nobody can tease him again | Throw his knitting needles and wool at Jack to make him stop | Pretend he was not knitting and hide it under his seat
test: Max says, "Bruh, scissors are scissors" ("bruh" means come on). Which is true? | Paper blunts fabric scissors, so they are kept for cloth | All scissors cut everything equally well | Fabric scissors are made for paper | Felt makes scissors sharper
test: Tom from Leeds thinks the paper fish was made by a printer. What is the correction? | Skilled cutters work by hand | Nobody in the world is able to cut paper that finely by hand | It was really cut by a robot in a factory in Xi'an | It was drawn with a pen
exam: Identify the principal reason for which thick masses of clay are liable to fracture during firing. | Trapped water becomes expanding steam | Thick clay is too heavy to be supported upon the shelves within the kiln | The kiln is insufficiently hot to fire clay of considerable thickness | Thick clay is poor quality
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the method by which pysanky are decorated? | Wax is applied to resist dye, repeated from lighter to darker colours | Pigment is applied with a fine brush in a single layer | Patterns are printed upon the egg by machine | Adhesive images are affixed to the shell
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of a hot glue gun by children. | Only under adult supervision | It may be used without supervision, being merely an adhesive for crafts | It presents no hazard whatsoever provided that it is used rapidly | Only adults may own one
exam: Ascertain the procedure most appropriate for joining two pieces of clay. | The scoring of both surfaces, application of slip, and pressing together | The pressing of one piece upon the other without preparation | The application of adhesive tape until the clay dries | The drying of both pieces prior to their combination
